      paragraphs: [
      `causing large cracks that broke into pieces perfect for the foundations of their new homes. The debris was either placed onto foundations, shaping logs into planks to fit their large frames, or used to reinforce structures. The first major innovation of this rebuilding effort was the underground corridors—vital for delivering messages and supplies swiftly. These tunnels kept streets open for commerce and provided covert pathways to rush into strategic locations. Supply outposts and construction sites could be reached quickly by underground routes, connecting trenches made by humans and Dalagbuan alike—routes for fighting back or delivering resources in a timely manner. "Seraya's brow furrowed as she examined the stubborn patch of soil before her. Harder than she'd anticipated—clay tangled with roots and stones—it threatened to halt her progress. Her hands, wrapped with strips of linen for comfort, trembled slightly with anticipation. She knew this was her moment, her chance to turn her idea into reality. Taking a deep breath, she steadied herself. The rough wooden handle, carved with her own careful design, felt familiar now—her own creation born from countless nights of thought and effort. She aligned the steel tip at the earth's base, then began to turn the handle. The drill squeaked against the soil, slow and resistant. Her muscles strained as she pushed against the stubborn earth, feeling the resistance biting back. For a moment, she thought her efforts were futile—the soil refused to yield. But she remembered her purpose: persistence. Adjusting her grip, she wrapped her hands more securely with linen. Another deep breath, then more forceful twisting. Suddenly, the steel tip bit into the earth, and the soil cracked`,
      `with a soft, satisfying sound. A narrow tunnel appeared—her first successful breakthrough. Her eyes widened with surprise and pride. She had done it. "Yes!" she whispered, a grin spreading across her face. Her arms burned from effort, but the taste of success was sweeter than she had imagined. Fueled by this victory, she powered through the stubborn patch, clay tumbling out like broken noodles behind her. Her Tikbalang helpers collected the debris, reinforcing her progress. With each breakthrough, she saw her people erecting wooden supports, ensuring she could advance without fear. From a distance, villagers and artisans watched her struggle and triumph. They cheered softly, awe and encouragement shining in their eyes. The young Tikbalang apprentice clapped his hands, eyes alight with admiration. Seraya wiped her brow, breathless yet exhilarated. She knew this was just the beginning—her invention could be refined, made faster and safer. But this small victory proved her idea worked, a testament to ingenuity and perseverance overcoming even the toughest earth. Using her strength and her mistress's knowledge, Seraya and her oathswarm rebuilt their city from the ashes. Though destruction had ended, the ruins still whispered stories of survival—hope reborn from the rubble, a future forged anew." Philippine Marines brought their craftsmanship, blending their styles into the city's architecture. Men and women chopped, carved, and nailed planks into structures—turning raw timber into homes and communal buildings. Despite the hard labor, hope shimmered in every hammer strike, every fitting piece of wood. The scars of war remained, fresh and raw, but the city was rising from the ashes.`,
      `Tensions flared when a Tikbalang, with his powerful build, pushed past a Marine carrying freshly cut timber, nearly knocking him over. Private Andrew froze in disbelief as the scene unfolded. Bea stepped forward swiftly, holding her steaming cup of black coffee, her face set with determination. The dark liquid billowed slightly as she spoke, her voice firm. "Hey, I saw that. You better apologize, or I'll report it to Alexa," she said sharply, her eyes locked on the Tikbalang. The creature looked confused. "What did I do?" he asked innocently. Andrew opened his mouth to speak, but Bea wasn't done. She stepped closer, cup raised threateningly. "You deliberately pushed him. And Andrew, you need to stand up for yourself. We've trained under Alexa—she won't tolerate disrespect." Andrew sighed, understanding her point. He stepped forward, shoulders squared. "I just want to make sure this doesn't happen again. I know our people hurt you—" Before he finished, Bea cut him off, her tone softening but remaining firm. "We're here to help. We're all trying to rebuild what was lost." Andrew looked down, dusting himself off. His wounds—inflicted when he fell amid the chaos—were visible. Bea gently dabbed at them with a napkin, her hands steady and caring. "Let's get you patched up," she said softly, applying ointment to soothe the cuts. "You need rest—you'll heal faster."`,
      `He nodded, grateful. "Thanks, Bea. I appreciate it." She smiled faintly, handing him back the cup. "Take it easy. We're in this together." "There better be," she replied, patting his back. "Get some rest—you'll help more that way." He smiled in return. "Thanks. I'll do my best." Bea watched him go, then turned back to the ongoing work. Despite the camaraderie, beneath the surface, tensions still lingered—remnants of the chaos and pain they endured. Their work an common area to talk and relax after a days work. One that was mostly rubble and ash but, soon will hold witness to plenty of history for the emerging city. Andrew leaned against a makeshift wall, soldiers nearby joking and teasing one another. "Could've kissed her," one grinned, nudging another. "We're here to repopulate," teased a second, causing laughter to ripple through the group. "Make kids." Andrew chuckled, feeling the weight of the day lift, if only for a moment. "I can do it," he said confidently, sipping his coffee. His friend patted his shoulder. "We bet on you—don't let us down." Andrew smirked. "Waiting for the right moment, huh?"`,
      `"Better hurry," his friend grinned. "She might find someone else." Their banter masked hope and determination. They knew their future depended on everyone pulling together—building a new life from the ruins. Meanwhile, Bea and others worked tirelessly, gathering debris to be crushed and mixed into clay bricks. The heavy hammers of Tikbalang smiths rang out, their bruised bodies testament to their dedication. Bea sighed, watching the progress. "We're making good headway," she said quietly. She joked, "Maybe we should help rebuild at our place, too." One Tikbalang grunted, continuing his work, unfazed. Bea frowned slightly. "They hate us, don't they?" she muttered, glancing at the other workers, who were treated with more respect. Yet for Alexa she picked up the stones and put them on the basket for what was to come. "Favoritism," she mumbled as she picked up the next one. "Nothing we can do about it." Her senior, Alexa, approached, hauling more rubble in her wheelbarrow. "The four of you earned this," Alexa said proudly. "We're all here to rebuild—that's what matters." Bea hesitated but nodded. "Yeah. We're all in this together but, that takes work... Plenty of it."`,
      `"Don't worry, Bea. We're new here, and the last ones they really hated are buried in the rubble. Just don't do what they did," her senior advised. Alexa pushed her wheelbarrow upward, debris raining down into the echoing crumbling up ahead. She added, "We both came here to rebuild. This is just another part of it." Bea nodded, feeling the hope. Her husband and the others were recovering better than ever. "Yes, that's true," Bea agreed. She looked forward to cooperation with the Dalagbuan—knowing their help would strengthen their efforts. "Don't slack off. Let's do this," she said, pushing herself to keep going. Soon, the city's ruins would be cleared, and new stores and homes would rise. The underground highways would be reinforced, becoming arteries for transporting goods, materials, and services—transforming their species' future. Later, behind closed doors, the most influential leaders gathered for secret talks. Robin opened the meeting with confidence. "Greetings, everyone. Let's begin our negotiations. Karduke, if anything's unclear, just ask. Our abilities are words—no need for letters." She laid a document on the table, smiling. The others looked on with anticipation. "We can read your letters, but you can't yet," Robin explained, emphasizing the language barrier and their ability to bridge it. Karduke nodded, relaxed. "Please, read it aloud—word by word."`,
      `Robin obliged, her voice steady as she recited the treaty. It outlined mutual trade, cooperation in defense, and shared resources—especially the strange material powering their mana engines. The Duke listened carefully. "We seek an equal partnership, not to be vassals," he said, eyes sharp but respectful. Robin briefed him on the mysterious material—its properties, the water ore's effects, a good means of trading and the potential for joint research. She explained their hopes for long-term cooperation, including studying creatures like rainbow spiders and the mana engine's secrets. "I propose we establish a research pact," Robin said. "Let's explore this world's mysteries—its gods, its creatures—and unlock its secrets together." Karduke expressed cautious optimism. "This could benefit us all." The Duke's interest grew. "I'd like to see those. They'd be valuable for trade." Robin nodded. "We can consider that. And we'd like to set up an embassy here." Karduke raised an eyebrow. "An embassy? For security?" The negotiations grew tense as Carl, a military leader, voiced concerns. "Four guards are not enough," he insisted. "Our people committed genocide against your kind. If we're to have an embassy, we need security—five or ten guards, at least." The Duke frowned. "If four can do the job, why not four?"`,
      `Carl's voice hardened. "Because even with ten, it's risky. Better to station three of our men—mercenaries—paid in goods—as protection." The Duke considered, then nodded. "Acceptable. An embassy with a small security force. We'll support this." The negotiations continued, covering security, land rights, and shared development. Robin stressed the importance of mutual trust and shared ambition. They finalized the documents, sealing their alliance. They signed the pact, each aware that the future depended on cooperation, trust, and shared ambition. s the talks drew to a close, Karduke handed her a carved dagger—a symbol of their enduring struggle. "Take care of this," he said. "And if you need help, call on us." Robin accepted the dagger, touched by its intricate carvings depicting battles and victories. "Thank you, Karduke. I will tell your story." He nodded. "Make sure your story is worth telling." As they departed, the leaders and workers alike felt a renewed sense of hope. The city was starting to rise—not just in stone, but in spirit.`,
      ]
